  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Friday)\nIssued at 115 PM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nActive southwest upper level flow continues with southeasterly \nsurface flow. Surface dew points range from the upper 50s in \nWyoming and the Black Hills to mid 60s across western South \nDakota. Low clouds eroded through the morning with surface heating\ndriving temperatures into the upper 70s and low 80s. SPC \nmesoanalysis and the 18z KUNR RAOB show 500-1000 J/kg MLCAPE \nacross the area with effective bulk shear around 30 to 40 knots. \nScattered convection is trying to develop across NW SD and NE WY, \nalthough coverage may be limited until a shortwave arrives later \nthis afternoon and early evening. Widespread severe weather is not\nanticipated, however isolated strong to severe storms are \npossible given the instability and shear parameter space.\n\nDaily chances for showers and thunderstorms will persist through the \nweekend into early next week. Upper level flow is forecast to become \nmore zonal/northwesterly early next week into mid week. Severe \npotential increases Sunday and Monday with ample instability and \nshear around 35 to 50 knots. Severe potential may stretch into \nTuesday, but timing of the wave and location of the surface front \nremain uncertain.\n\nLonger range guidance shows a strong area of high pressure building \nover the SW US by end of next week into the weekend with \ntemperatures trending warmer for our area.",
